 "title": "Bug Hunt 4 - Shape Party (inheritance bugs)",
 "body": "<p><strong>Goal:</strong> fix the 3 bugs so 4 hexagons and 3 diamonds (squares standing on a corner) appear in 7 colors.</p>\n<p><strong>How to hunt</strong> - the bugs show up one after another:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Press <strong>Run</strong>. The window opens, but NOTHING is drawn and there is no error. A silent bug! Click <strong>Debug</strong> and <strong>Step into</strong> <code>draw_party</code>. Does Thonny ever go inside a <code>draw</code> method? (Hint: how do you CALL a method?)</li>\n<li>Fix it and run again. Now it crashes. Read the last line of the red error. Which attribute is missing? Which <code>__init__</code> should have set it?</li>\n<li>Fix it and run again. Look closely: are the diamonds really diamonds, or plain dots? Compare the method names in <code>Diamond</code> and <code>Shape</code> letter by letter.</li>\n</ol>\n",
 "hints": [
  {
   "label": "Hint 1",
   "title": "Where to look",
   "html": "<p>Run it. The window opens but nothing is drawn, and there is no error. Look at the function that loops over the shapes and asks each one to draw. How do you normally tell Python to run a method?</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "label": "Hint 2",
   "title": "Try this",
   "html": "<p>After the first fix, a crash appears. Read the last line of the red error. It names a missing attribute. Ask which <code>__init__</code> should have set it, and what a child class can do to also run its parent's <code>__init__</code>.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "label": "Hint 3",
   "title": "Almost there",
   "html": "<p>For the last bug, compare the method names in <code>Diamond</code> and <code>Shape</code> letter by letter. A child class replaces a parent method only when the names match exactly. For the silent bug, step into <code>draw_party</code> and watch whether Thonny ever goes inside a <code>draw</code> method.</p>\n"
  }
